{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent medieval ruler who lived from approximately 1140 to July 16 1187. He was the younger son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Tripoli. Raymond III played a crucial role in the political and military landscape of the Levant during the Crusader era.\n\n**Early life and background**\nRaymond III was born around 1140 into the House of Toulouse which had strong ties to the Crusader states. His father Raymond II was a powerful prince who had married Hodierna the daughter of Baldwin II of Jerusalem. This family connection positioned Raymond III within the elite nobility of the Crusader states from an early age. His upbringing exposed him to the complex politics and warfare that defined the region during that turbulent period. Raymond III was also the brother of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch a key figure in the Crusader world.\n\n**Military career and leadership**\n*highlighted section part 1*\nRaymond III gained prominence through his military prowess and political acumen. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father Raymond II passed away. His reign was marked by a series of conflicts with both Muslim forces and rival Christian factions. One of his most significant military engagements was during the Siege of Harim in 1159. Alongside Bohemond III he played a pivotal role in defending the city against Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler who was seeking to expand his influence in the region. This victory stabilized the northern front of the Crusader states for a period.\n\nRaymond III was also involved in political maneuvering to strengthen his position. He married Eschiva of Bures who brought additional territories and alliances. Their marriage produced two sons Raymond IV and Bohemond IV who would later inherit key Crusader territories. His ability to navigate the shifting alliances and power dynamics of the Crusader states was a testament to his leadership skills.\n\n**Later years and death**\nRaymond III continued to be an active participant in the defense of the Crusader states against the growing threat posed by Saladin. In 1187 he joined the forces of Guy of Lusignan at the Battle of Hattin a decisive conflict that marked a turning point in the Crusades. The defeat at Hattin resulted in the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin shortly thereafter. Raymond III died of dysentery on July 16 1187 while still engaged in efforts to resist Saladin’s advance.\n\nAlthough Raymond III's death was a blow to the Crusader states his legacy persisted through his sons and his contributions to the defense of Tripoli. His reign highlighted the challenges faced by the Crusader states in maintaining their foothold in the Levant amid the rising power of the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin.\n\n**Family and succession**\nRaymond III’s family connections were integral to his political influence. He was married to Eschiva of Bures whose family had ties to the French nobility. Their sons Raymond IV and Bohemond IV both inherited significant titles and territories. Raymond IV succeeded as Count of Tripoli while Bohemond IV became Prince of Antioch. These familial links ensured that Raymond III’s influence extended beyond his own reign shaping the future of the Crusader states for generations.\n\nThe historical accounts of Raymond III portray a ruler who was both a capable military leader and a skilled political operator. His efforts to defend Tripoli and maintain the balance of power in the Levant reflect the broader struggles of the Crusader states during the 12th century. Raymond III’s life and deeds remain a significant chapter in the history of the medieval Near East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607